    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Custom Resource is a data structure that extends the Kubernetes API. An Operator is a controller that watches custom resources and takes actions to reconcile the desired state. Operators use CRDs to represent the application configuration.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a custom resource and an operator and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A Custom Resource is a data structure that extends the Kubernetes API.\n2. An Operator is a controller that watches custom resources and takes actions to reconcile the desired state.\n3. Operators use CRDs to represent the application configuration.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A Custom Resource is a data structure that extends the Kubernetes API.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A Custom Resource is a data structure that extends the Kubernetes API.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
