    question: "What is a high-availability (HA) Kubernetes cluster?",
    slug: "ha-kubernetes",
    shortDescription: "Explain HA setup.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA highly available Kubernetes cluster removes major single points of failure by running multiple control-plane components and using an appropriate highly available etcd configuration. Multiple API servers are normally placed behind a load balancer, while etcd requires quorum and should have an odd number of members in typical deployments. HA improves control-plane availability, but it does not automatically make applications highly available; workloads also need multiple replicas, appropriate scheduling, storage, and disruption policies.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a high-availability (HA) Kubernetes cluster accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A highly available Kubernetes cluster removes major single points of failure by running multiple control-plane components and using an appropriate highly available etcd configuration.\n2. Multiple API servers are normally placed behind a load balancer, while etcd requires quorum and should have an odd number of members in typical deployments.\n3. HA improves control-plane availability, but it does not automatically make applications highly available; workloads also need multiple replicas, appropriate scheduling, storage, and disruption policies.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A highly available Kubernetes cluster removes major single points of failure by running multiple control-plane components and using an appropriate highly available etcd configuration.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A highly available Kubernetes cluster removes major single points of failure by running multiple control-plane components and using an appropriate highly available etcd configuration.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
