    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nGitOps is an operating model in which Git stores the declarative desired state for applications and infrastructure, while an agent in or around the cluster continuously reconciles the actual cluster state with that desired state. With Kubernetes, tools such as Argo CD or Flux can detect Git changes, apply manifests, and report drift. The benefit is an auditable, reviewable deployment process, but secrets, emergency changes, drift, and repository access still need explicit operational controls.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain GitOps and how does it work with Kubernetes accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. GitOps is an operating model in which Git stores the declarative desired state for applications and infrastructure, while an agent in or around the cluster continuously reconciles the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n2. With Kubernetes, tools such as Argo CD or Flux can detect Git changes, apply manifests, and report drift.\n3. The benefit is an auditable, reviewable deployment process, but secrets, emergency changes, drift, and repository access still need explicit operational controls.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. GitOps is an operating model in which Git stores the declarative desired state for applications and infrastructure, while an agent in or around the cluster continuously reconciles the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. GitOps is an operating model in which Git stores the declarative desired state for applications and infrastructure, while an agent in or around the cluster continuously reconciles the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
