    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA Job runs a task once. A CronJob runs a Job on a schedule (e.g., every 5 minutes). CronJob uses cron expressions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a job and a cronjob in terms of scheduling and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A Job runs a task once.\n2. A CronJob runs a Job on a schedule (e.g., every 5 minutes).\n3. CronJob uses cron expressions.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A Job runs a task once.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A Job runs a task once.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
