    slug: "node-failure-vs-eviction",
    shortDescription: "Explain the causes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA node failure is a node going down (e.g., power loss). Pods on that node are considered failed and will be recreated elsewhere. A Pod eviction is a deliberate process where the node evicts Pods due to resource pressure or node maintenance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a node failure and a pod eviction and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A node failure is a node going down (e.g., power loss).\n2. Pods on that node are considered failed and will be recreated elsewhere.\n3. A Pod eviction is a deliberate process where the node evicts Pods due to resource pressure or node maintenance.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A node failure is a node going down (e.g., power loss).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A node failure is a node going down (e.g., power loss).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
