    slug: "blue-green-k8s",
    shortDescription: "Explain the strategy.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse two separate Deployments (blue and green) and a Service with a selector. Initially, the service points to blue. To switch, update the service's selector to point to green. This is a simple way to do blue-green.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you perform a blue-green deployment in kubernetes, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use two separate Deployments (blue and green) and a Service with a selector.\n2. Initially, the service points to blue.\n3. To switch, update the service's selector to point to green.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nUpdate the image tag in a Deployment and let the controller roll out replacement Pods while readiness checks prevent unready Pods from receiving traffic.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use two separate Deployments (blue and green) and a Service with a selector.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
