    question: "What is the startup probe and when would you use it?",
    slug: "startup-probe",
    shortDescription: "Explain the probe.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA startup probe is designed for containers that need significant time to initialize. While the startup probe has not succeeded, Kubernetes does not run the container's liveness and readiness probes. Once startup succeeds, the normal liveness and readiness checks take over. This prevents a slow-starting application from being repeatedly restarted by an aggressive liveness probe during initialization.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the startup probe and when would you use it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A startup probe is designed for containers that need significant time to initialize.\n2. While the startup probe has not succeeded, Kubernetes does not run the container's liveness and readiness probes.\n3. Once startup succeeds, the normal liveness and readiness checks take over.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nExpose a health endpoint and use Kubernetes readiness to control whether the Pod receives traffic; use liveness only for failures where restarting the container is actually the desired recovery.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A startup probe is designed for containers that need significant time to initialize.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
