    question: "What is the difference between a hard link and a soft (symbolic) link?",
    slug: "hard-link-vs-soft-link",
    shortDescription: "Explain inode vs pathname linking.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA hard link is a direct reference to an inode; it points to the same data blocks as the original file. A soft link (symbolic link) is a separate file that points to the pathname of another file. Hard links cannot span filesystems; soft links can. A hard link is another directory entry for the same inode, so both names share the same file contents and metadata such as ownership and mode. A symbolic link has its own inode and stores a target pathname, so it can become dangling when the target disappears.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a hard link and a soft (symbolic) link and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A hard link is a direct reference to an inode; it points to the same data blocks as the original file.\n2. A soft link (symbolic link) is a separate file that points to the pathname of another file.\n3. Hard links cannot span filesystems; soft links can.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A hard link is a direct reference to an inode; it points to the same data blocks as the original file.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A hard link is a direct reference to an inode; it points to the same data blocks as the original file.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
