    question: "What is the difference between `kill` and `kill -9`?",
    slug: "kill-vs-kill-9",
    shortDescription: "Explain signal handling.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`kill` sends a SIGTERM (15) signal, allowing the process to clean up and exit gracefully. `kill -9` sends a SIGKILL (9) signal, which forcibly terminates the process immediately without cleanup. SIGTERM can be caught, allowing cleanup, while SIGKILL cannot be caught or ignored. A good operational sequence is to try SIGTERM first, inspect whether the process exits, and use SIGKILL only when necessary because it prevents application-level cleanup.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `kill` and `kill -9` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `kill` sends a SIGTERM (15) signal, allowing the process to clean up and exit gracefully.\n2. `kill -9` sends a SIGKILL (9) signal, which forcibly terminates the process immediately without cleanup.\n3. SIGTERM can be caught, allowing cleanup, while SIGKILL cannot be caught or ignored.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `kill` sends a SIGTERM (15) signal, allowing the process to clean up and exit gracefully.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `kill` sends a SIGTERM (15) signal, allowing the process to clean up and exit gracefully.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
