    question: "Explain the concept of journaling in Linux filesystems.",
    slug: "journaling",
    shortDescription: "Describe how journaling works.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJournaling is a technique where filesystem changes are first written to a log (journal) before being committed to the main filesystem. In case of a crash, the journal can be replayed to recover the filesystem to a consistent state, avoiding lengthy `fsck` runs. A journal records intended filesystem metadata changes so recovery can replay or discard incomplete transactions after a crash. The exact durability guarantee depends on the filesystem's journal mode and storage/cache behavior, so journaling should not be presented as a substitute for backups.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the concept of journaling in Linux filesystems. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Journaling is a technique where filesystem changes are first written to a log (journal) before being committed to the main filesystem.\n2. In case of a crash, the journal can be replayed to recover the filesystem to a consistent state, avoiding lengthy `fsck` runs.\n3. A journal records intended filesystem metadata changes so recovery can replay or discard incomplete transactions after a crash.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Journaling is a technique where filesystem changes are first written to a log (journal) before being committed to the main filesystem.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. Journaling is a technique where filesystem changes are first written to a log (journal) before being committed to the main filesystem.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
