    question: "Explain the OSI model and how TCP/IP maps to it.",
    slug: "osi-model",
    shortDescription: "Describe the 7-layer model.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application. TCP/IP maps to this: Network Interface (L1-2), Internet (L3), Transport (L4), and Application (L5-7). OSI is mainly a conceptual model, not a strict implementation map. TCP/IP commonly groups link functions into the network-access layer and session/presentation/application concerns into the application layer, so the mapping is approximate rather than one-to-one.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the OSI model and how TCP/IP maps to it. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.\n2. TCP/IP maps to this: Network Interface (L1-2), Internet (L3), Transport (L4), and Application (L5-7).\n3. OSI is mainly a conceptual model, not a strict implementation map.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. The OSI model has 7 layers: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
