    question: "How does ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) work?",
    slug: "arp",
    shortDescription: "Explain how IP addresses are resolved to MAC addresses.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nARP resolves an IP address to a physical MAC address on a local network. A host broadcasts an ARP request asking 'Who has this IP?'. The owner replies with its MAC address, which is cached in the ARP table (`arp -a`). ARP is used for IPv4 on a local link; IPv6 uses Neighbor Discovery instead. If the destination is outside the local subnet, the host resolves the MAC address of its next-hop gateway rather than the remote host.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. ARP resolves an IP address to a physical MAC address on a local network.\n2. A host broadcasts an ARP request asking 'Who has this IP?'.\n3. The owner replies with its MAC address, which is cached in the ARP table (`arp -a`).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. ARP resolves an IP address to a physical MAC address on a local network.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. ARP resolves an IP address to a physical MAC address on a local network.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
