    slug: "hosts-resolv",
    shortDescription: "Explain local DNS resolution.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`/etc/hosts` is a local static mapping of hostnames to IP addresses, checked before DNS. `/etc/resolv.conf` specifies DNS nameservers and search domains for domain name resolution. Hostname resolution behavior can be influenced by `/etc/nsswitch.conf`, so `/etc/hosts` and DNS are not universally ordered simply because both exist. `/etc/resolv.conf` supplies resolver configuration, often through a system service such as systemd-resolved or NetworkManager.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the purpose of `/etc/hosts` and `/etc/resolv.conf` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `/etc/hosts` is a local static mapping of hostnames to IP addresses, checked before DNS.\n2. `/etc/resolv.conf` specifies DNS nameservers and search domains for domain name resolution.\n3. Hostname resolution behavior can be influenced by `/etc/nsswitch.conf`, so `/etc/hosts` and DNS are not universally ordered simply because both exist.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `/etc/hosts` is a local static mapping of hostnames to IP addresses, checked before DNS.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `/etc/hosts` is a local static mapping of hostnames to IP addresses, checked before DNS.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
