    slug: "iptables-nftables",
    shortDescription: "Describe the Linux firewall.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`iptables` (and its successor `nftables`) is a userspace utility for configuring the Linux kernel netfilter firewall. It uses chains of rules to filter, NAT, and mangle packets. `nftables` offers a simpler syntax and better performance. Netfilter is the kernel packet-processing framework; `iptables` and `nft` are user-space tools that configure rules. On many current distributions, the `iptables` command may be an nftables-backed compatibility interface, so the actual backend should be checked before assuming a legacy ruleset.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe how `iptables`/`nftables` work. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `iptables` (and its successor `nftables`) is a userspace utility for configuring the Linux kernel netfilter firewall.\n2. It uses chains of rules to filter, NAT, and mangle packets.\n3. `nftables` offers a simpler syntax and better performance.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `iptables` (and its successor `nftables`) is a userspace utility for configuring the Linux kernel netfilter firewall.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. `iptables` (and its successor `nftables`) is a userspace utility for configuring the Linux kernel netfilter firewall.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
