    question: "How does DHCP work in Linux?",
    slug: "dhcp",
    shortDescription: "Explain dynamic IP assignment.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) automatically assigns IP addresses and network configuration to clients. The client broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER, the server offers an IP (DHCPOFFER), the client requests it (DHCPREQUEST), and the server acknowledges (DHCPACK). DHCP normally follows Discover, Offer, Request, and Acknowledge. Besides an address, it can provide options such as subnet mask/prefix, default gateway, DNS servers, lease time, and domain information.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain DHCP work in Linux accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) automatically assigns IP addresses and network configuration to clients.\n2. The client broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER, the server offers an IP (DHCPOFFER), the client requests it (DHCPREQUEST), and the server acknowledges (DHCPACK).\n3. DHCP normally follows Discover, Offer, Request, and Acknowledge.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) automatically assigns IP addresses and network configuration to clients.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) automatically assigns IP addresses and network configuration to clients.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
