    question: "What is SELinux and how does it work?",
    slug: "selinux",
    shortDescription: "Explain mandatory access control.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSELinux (Security-Enhanced Linux) is a Linux kernel module that provides Mandatory Access Control (MAC). It enforces security policies that define which processes can access which files, directories, and ports. It operates in three modes: Enforcing, Permissive, and Disabled. SELinux decisions are based on security labels and policy, not simply on pathnames. Permissive mode logs policy denials without enforcing them, while enforcing mode blocks disallowed access; disabling SELinux is different from permissive mode.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain SELinux and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SELinux (Security-Enhanced Linux) is a Linux kernel module that provides Mandatory Access Control (MAC).\n2. It enforces security policies that define which processes can access which files, directories, and ports.\n3. It operates in three modes: Enforcing, Permissive, and Disabled.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. SELinux (Security-Enhanced Linux) is a Linux kernel module that provides Mandatory Access Control (MAC).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. SELinux (Security-Enhanced Linux) is a Linux kernel module that provides Mandatory Access Control (MAC).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
