    slug: "fail2ban",
    shortDescription: "Explain brute-force protection.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFail2ban is an intrusion prevention tool that monitors log files (e.g., `/var/log/auth.log`) for repeated failed login attempts. It then temporarily bans the offending IP addresses using firewall rules (`iptables` or `firewalld`). Fail2ban is log-driven and usually applies temporary bans through a firewall or other action. It complements, rather than replaces, strong authentication, rate limiting, patching, MFA where supported, and network-level controls.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Fail2ban and how is it used accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Fail2ban is an intrusion prevention tool that monitors log files (e.g., `/var/log/auth.log`) for repeated failed login attempts.\n2. It then temporarily bans the offending IP addresses using firewall rules (`iptables` or `firewalld`).\n3. Fail2ban is log-driven and usually applies temporary bans through a firewall or other action.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Fail2ban is an intrusion prevention tool that monitors log files (e.g., `/var/log/auth.log`) for repeated failed login attempts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Fail2ban is an intrusion prevention tool that monitors log files (e.g., `/var/log/auth.log`) for repeated failed login attempts.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
