    shortDescription: "Walk through the diagnostic steps.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1. Use `top` or `htop` to identify CPU-hungry processes. 2. Use `ps aux --sort=-%cpu` to list processes by CPU usage. 3. Check for runaway processes, infinite loops, or high context switching. 4. Use `strace` to trace system calls of the offending process. Start with process-level evidence and determine whether the CPU is user time, system time, or spent elsewhere. A high-CPU process can be application work, a tight loop, excessive system calls, or contention; profiling is preferable to killing it immediately.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you troubleshoot a system with high cpu usage, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1.\n2. Use `top` or `htop` to identify CPU-hungry processes.\n3. 2.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. 1.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 1.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
