    focus: null,
    question: "What is the difference between I/O wait and CPU utilization?",
    slug: "iowait-vs-cpu",
    shortDescription: "Explain the difference.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCPU utilization is the percentage of time the CPU is busy executing instructions. I/O wait is the percentage of time the CPU is idle waiting for I/O operations (disk, network) to complete. High I/O wait indicates storage or network bottlenecks. I/O wait is a CPU accounting state associated with idle CPU time while tasks are waiting on I/O; it is not itself a disk-utilization percentage. High iowait is a clue, not a diagnosis, so correlate it with device latency, queue depth, throughput, and application behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between i/o wait and cpu utilization and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. CPU utilization is the percentage of time the CPU is busy executing instructions.\n2. I/O wait is the percentage of time the CPU is idle waiting for I/O operations (disk, network) to complete.\n3. High I/O wait indicates storage or network bottlenecks.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. CPU utilization is the percentage of time the CPU is busy executing instructions.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. CPU utilization is the percentage of time the CPU is busy executing instructions.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
