    slug: "disk-io-troubleshoot",
    shortDescription: "Explain I/O monitoring tools.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse `iostat -x 1` to monitor disk I/O statistics (await, svctm, util). Use `iotop` to see per-process I/O usage. Use `blktrace` for detailed block I/O tracing. Check for high `%util` or high `await` values. `iostat -xz 1` can show device utilization, throughput, request sizes, queueing and latency-related statistics. High utilization alone does not identify the bottleneck; compare latency and queue depth with workload expectations and inspect per-process I/O.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you diagnose disk i/o performance issues, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use `iostat -x 1` to monitor disk I/O statistics (await, svctm, util).\n2. Use `iotop` to see per-process I/O usage.\n3. Use `blktrace` for detailed block I/O tracing.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use `iostat -x 1` to monitor disk I/O statistics (await, svctm, util).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use `iostat -x 1` to monitor disk I/O statistics (await, svctm, util).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
