    question: "What is the difference between `top` and `htop`?",
    slug: "top-vs-htop",
    shortDescription: "Compare the process monitoring tools.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`top` is the standard system monitoring tool. `htop` is an improved version with a more user-friendly interface, color coding, mouse support, easier process killing, and tree view. `htop` also shows more detailed system information. Both are views over process and system statistics; the main difference is interaction and presentation, not fundamentally different kernel data. For automation and minimal systems, `top` is commonly available, while `htop` is convenient for interactive exploration.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `top` and `htop` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `top` is the standard system monitoring tool.\n2. `htop` is an improved version with a more user-friendly interface, color coding, mouse support, easier process killing, and tree view.\n3. `htop` also shows more detailed system information.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `top` is the standard system monitoring tool.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `top` is the standard system monitoring tool.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
