    question: "What is the difference between `bash` and `sh`?",
    slug: "bash-vs-sh",
    shortDescription: "Compare the shells.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell. `bash` (Bourne Again Shell) is an enhanced version with additional features like command history, job control, and bash-specific syntax (e.g., arrays, `[[ ]]`). `/bin/sh` is a command name whose implementation varies by distribution; it may be dash, bash in POSIX mode, or another shell. A script using Bash-only features should use `#!/usr/bin/env bash` or an appropriate Bash path rather than assuming `sh` supports them.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `bash` and `sh` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell.\n2. `bash` (Bourne Again Shell) is an enhanced version with additional features like command history, job control, and bash-specific syntax (e.g., arrays, `[[ ]]`).\n3. `/bin/sh` is a command name whose implementation varies by distribution; it may be dash, bash in POSIX mode, or another shell.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell. `bash` (Bourne Again Shell) is an enhanced version with additional features like command history, job control, and bash-specific syntax (e.g., arrays, `[[ ]]`). `/bin/sh` is a command name whose implementation varies by distribution; it may be dash, bash in POSIX mode, or another shell. A script using Bash-only features should use `#!/usr/bin/env bash` or an appropriate Bash path rather than assuming `sh` supports them.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `bash` and `sh` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell.\n2. `bash` (Bourne Again Shell) is an enhanced version with additional features like command history, job control, and bash-specific syntax (e.g., arrays, `[[ ]]`).\n3. `/bin/sh` is a command name whose implementation varies by distribution; it may be dash, bash in POSIX mode, or another shell.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `sh` is the original Bourne shell, a simple POSIX-compliant shell.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
