    question: "What is the difference between `$*` and `$@` in bash?",
    slug: "dollar-star-vs-dollar-at",
    shortDescription: "Explain positional parameter expansion.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`$*` expands all positional parameters as a single word (e.g., `\\\"$1 $2 $3\\\"`). `$@` expands each positional parameter as a separate word (e.g., `\\\"$1\\\" \\\"$2\\\" \\\"$3\\\"`). `$@` is generally safer for preserving arguments. The key difference appears when they are quoted: `\"$@\"` expands to one word per positional argument, preserving argument boundaries, while `\"$*\"` joins the arguments using the first character of `IFS`. Unquoted forms undergo additional word splitting and globbing and are usually unsafe.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `$*` and `$@` in bash and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `$*` expands all positional parameters as a single word (e.g., `\\\"$1 $2 $3\\\"`).\n2. `$@` expands each positional parameter as a separate word (e.g., `\\\"$1\\\" \\\"$2\\\" \\\"$3\\\"`).\n3. `$@` is generally safer for preserving arguments.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `$*` expands all positional parameters as a single word (e.g., `\\\"$1 $2 $3\\\"`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `$*` expands all positional parameters as a single word (e.g., `\\\"$1 $2 $3\\\"`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
