    question: "How do you redirect stdout and stderr to different files?",
    slug: "redirect-stdout-stderr",
    shortDescription: "Explain I/O redirection.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`command > stdout.log 2> stderr.log` redirects stdout to stdout.log and stderr to stderr.log. `command &> output.log` redirects both to the same file. File descriptor 1 is stdout and descriptor 2 is stderr. Redirection order matters: `>out 2>&1` sends both to `out`, while `2>&1 >out` leaves stderr pointing to the original stdout destination.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you redirect stdout and stderr to different files, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `command > stdout.log 2> stderr.log` redirects stdout to stdout.log and stderr to stderr.log.\n2. `command &> output.log` redirects both to the same file.\n3. File descriptor 1 is stdout and descriptor 2 is stderr.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the operation in the order you would actually perform it: prerequisite, main action, verification, and the important failure case.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. `command > stdout.log 2> stderr.log` redirects stdout to stdout.log and stderr to stderr.log.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `command > stdout.log 2> stderr.log` redirects stdout to stdout.log and stderr to stderr.log.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
