    question: "How do you create a systemd service?",
    slug: "create-systemd-service",
    shortDescription: "Explain service unit creation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCreate a file `/etc/systemd/system/my-service.service` with:\\n```\\n[Unit]\\nDescription=My Service\\nAfter=network.target\\n\\n[Service]\\nExecStart=/usr/bin/my-service\\nRestart=always\\n\\n[Install]\\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\\n```\\nThen run `systemctl daemon-reload` and `systemctl enable --now my-service`. After creating or changing a unit, `systemctl daemon-reload` makes systemd reread unit definitions. `enable --now` both configures boot-time activation and starts the service immediately; `Restart=` should be chosen carefully to avoid restart loops.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you create a systemd service, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Create a file `/etc/systemd/system/my-service.service` with:\\n```\\n[Unit]\\nDescription=My Service\\nAfter=network.target\\n\\n[Service]\\nExecStart=/usr/bin/my-service\\nRestart=always\\n\\n[Install]\\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\\n```\\nThen run `systemctl daemon-reload` and `systemctl enable --now my-service`.\n2. After creating or changing a unit, `systemctl daemon-reload` makes systemd reread unit definitions.\n3. `enable --now` both configures boot-time activation and starts the service immediately; `Restart=` should be chosen carefully to avoid restart loops.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Create a file `/etc/systemd/system/my-service.service` with:\\n```\\n[Unit]\\nDescription=My Service\\nAfter=network.target\\n\\n[Service]\\nExecStart=/usr/bin/my-service\\nRestart=always\\n\\n[Install]\\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\\n```\\nThen run `systemctl daemon-reload` and `systemctl enable --now my-service`.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Create a file `/etc/systemd/system/my-service.service` with:\\n```\\n[Unit]\\nDescription=My Service\\nAfter=network.target\\n\\n[Service]\\nExecStart=/usr/bin/my-service\\nRestart=always\\n\\n[Install]\\nWantedBy=multi-user.target\\n```\\nThen run `systemctl daemon-reload` and `systemctl enable --now my-service`.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
