    focus: null,
    question: "What is the difference between `systemctl start`, `enable`, and `restart`?",
    slug: "systemctl-commands",
    shortDescription: "Explain systemctl operations.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`systemctl start` starts a service immediately. `systemctl enable` configures the service to start at boot. `systemctl restart` stops and starts the service (for applying configuration changes). `systemctl reload` reloads the service configuration without restarting. `start` changes current runtime state, `enable` changes boot-time linkage, and `restart` replaces the running process. `reload` is application-dependent and only works when the service supports reloading its configuration without a full restart.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `systemctl start`, `enable`, and `restart` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `systemctl start` starts a service immediately.\n2. `systemctl enable` configures the service to start at boot.\n3. `systemctl restart` stops and starts the service (for applying configuration changes).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `systemctl start` starts a service immediately.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `systemctl start` starts a service immediately.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
