    question: "What is the difference between a spinlock and a mutex in the Linux kernel?",
    slug: "spinlock-vs-mutex",
    shortDescription: "Explain kernel synchronization primitives.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA spinlock is a busy-waiting lock that spins (repeatedly checks) until it acquires the lock. It is used in interrupt contexts where sleeping is not allowed. A mutex is a sleeping lock; if the lock is held, the thread sleeps and is woken up when the lock is released. Mutexes are used in process contexts. A spinlock is appropriate for very short critical sections where sleeping is impossible, including some interrupt-related contexts. A mutex can sleep and therefore cannot be used where the current context cannot block. The right choice depends on context and expected lock hold time.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a spinlock and a mutex in the linux kernel and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A spinlock is a busy-waiting lock that spins (repeatedly checks) until it acquires the lock.\n2. It is used in interrupt contexts where sleeping is not allowed.\n3. A mutex is a sleeping lock; if the lock is held, the thread sleeps and is woken up when the lock is released.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A spinlock is a busy-waiting lock that spins (repeatedly checks) until it acquires the lock.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A spinlock is a busy-waiting lock that spins (repeatedly checks) until it acquires the lock.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
