    question: "What is a `softirq` and how does it differ from a `tasklet`?",
    slug: "softirq-vs-tasklet",
    shortDescription: "Explain deferred interrupt processing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSoftirqs are kernel mechanisms for deferring work from interrupt handlers to a later time, running in interrupt context. Tasklets are a simpler, more limited form of softirqs that are non-reentrant (only one instance runs at a time). Tasklets are built on top of softirqs. Softirqs can run concurrently on multiple CPUs, while traditional tasklets serialize execution of the same tasklet instance. Tasklets have been deprecated in newer kernel development in favor of other mechanisms such as workqueues or threaded approaches where sleeping is needed.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a `softirq` and how does it differ from a `tasklet` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Softirqs are kernel mechanisms for deferring work from interrupt handlers to a later time, running in interrupt context.\n2. Tasklets are a simpler, more limited form of softirqs that are non-reentrant (only one instance runs at a time).\n3. Tasklets are built on top of softirqs.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Softirqs are kernel mechanisms for deferring work from interrupt handlers to a later time, running in interrupt context.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Softirqs are kernel mechanisms for deferring work from interrupt handlers to a later time, running in interrupt context.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
