    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen a hardware interrupt occurs, the CPU saves the current state and jumps to the interrupt handler. The handler does minimal work, acknowledges the interrupt, and schedules a softirq or tasklet for deferred processing. The kernel then returns to the interrupted process. The architecture enters the registered interrupt handler, which should do only the urgent work that cannot be deferred. Longer processing can be moved to an appropriate deferred mechanism, reducing interrupt latency and keeping the system responsive.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the Linux kernel handle interrupts accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. When a hardware interrupt occurs, the CPU saves the current state and jumps to the interrupt handler.\n2. The handler does minimal work, acknowledges the interrupt, and schedules a softirq or tasklet for deferred processing.\n3. The kernel then returns to the interrupted process.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. When a hardware interrupt occurs, the CPU saves the current state and jumps to the interrupt handler.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. When a hardware interrupt occurs, the CPU saves the current state and jumps to the interrupt handler.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
