    question: "What are Linux namespaces and what types exist?",
    slug: "linux-namespaces",
    shortDescription: "Explain kernel-level isolation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNamespaces isolate process trees, network interfaces, and other resources. Types include: `pid` (process IDs), `net` (network), `mnt` (mount points), `uts` (hostname), `ipc` (IPC), `user` (user IDs), and `cgroup` (control groups). Namespaces isolate specific views of kernel resources rather than creating a separate kernel. The major types include mount, PID, network, UTS, IPC, user, cgroup, and time namespaces; containers combine several of them for isolation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Namespaces isolate process trees, network interfaces, and other resources.\n2. Types include: `pid` (process IDs), `net` (network), `mnt` (mount points), `uts` (hostname), `ipc` (IPC), `user` (user IDs), and `cgroup` (control groups).\n3. Namespaces isolate specific views of kernel resources rather than creating a separate kernel.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Namespaces isolate process trees, network interfaces, and other resources.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nOn a Linux host, what are linux namespaces and what types exist matters when the operating system’s process, filesystem, network, or permission behavior affects service reliability. Verify the actual system state with the relevant diagnostic tools before changing configuration.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
