    slug: "cgroups",
    shortDescription: "Explain resource management.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCgroups are a Linux kernel feature that limits, accounts for, and isolates resource usage (CPU, memory, disk I/O, network) of process groups. They are the foundation for container resource management (e.g., Docker, Kubernetes). Cgroups organize processes hierarchically and let controllers account for and constrain resources such as memory and CPU. Modern Linux commonly uses cgroup v2, which provides a unified hierarchy and controller interface.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Cgroups are a Linux kernel feature that limits, accounts for, and isolates resource usage (CPU, memory, disk I/O, network) of process groups.\n2. They are the foundation for container resource management (e.g., Docker, Kubernetes).\n3. Cgroups organize processes hierarchically and let controllers account for and constrain resources such as memory and CPU.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Cgroups are a Linux kernel feature that limits, accounts for, and isolates resource usage (CPU, memory, disk I/O, network) of process groups.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nOn a Linux host, what are control groups (cgroups) and how are they used matters when the operating system’s process, filesystem, network, or permission behavior affects service reliability. Verify the actual system state with the relevant diagnostic tools before changing configuration.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
