    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDocker containers share the host kernel and are lightweight, starting in seconds. Virtual machines have their own full OS and kernel, providing stronger isolation but higher overhead and slower startup. A container image contains user-space files and metadata but does not contain a separate kernel. A VM boots a guest operating system and kernel, which generally increases overhead but can provide a stronger isolation boundary.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between docker and a virtual machine and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Docker containers share the host kernel and are lightweight, starting in seconds.\n2. Virtual machines have their own full OS and kernel, providing stronger isolation but higher overhead and slower startup.\n3. A container image contains user-space files and metadata but does not contain a separate kernel.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Docker containers share the host kernel and are lightweight, starting in seconds.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Docker containers share the host kernel and are lightweight, starting in seconds.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
