    slug: "xfs-vs-ext4",
    shortDescription: "Compare the filesystems.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nXFS is a high-performance 64-bit journaling filesystem, optimized for large files and parallel I/O. ext4 is the default Linux filesystem, offering good performance and backward compatibility. XFS supports larger file sizes and volumes than ext4. Both are journaling filesystems with different design strengths. XFS is particularly strong for large files, large filesystems, and parallel workloads; ext4 is widely supported and flexible. Neither should be selected solely by theoretical maximum size—workload, tooling, recovery requirements, and distribution support matter.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between xfs and ext4 and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. XFS is a high-performance 64-bit journaling filesystem, optimized for large files and parallel I/O.\n2. ext4 is the default Linux filesystem, offering good performance and backward compatibility.\n3. XFS supports larger file sizes and volumes than ext4.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. XFS is a high-performance 64-bit journaling filesystem, optimized for large files and parallel I/O.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. XFS is a high-performance 64-bit journaling filesystem, optimized for large files and parallel I/O.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
