    slug: "journald",
    shortDescription: "Explain systemd's logging daemon.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`journald` is systemd's logging daemon that collects and stores logs in a binary format (`/var/log/journal/`). It provides structured logging, indexing, and integration with `systemctl` and `journalctl`. journald stores structured journal entries and supports filtering by unit, boot, priority, time, and fields. Persistence is configuration-dependent; a system may keep logs only in volatile storage unless persistent journal storage is enabled.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the purpose of the `journald` service accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `journald` is systemd's logging daemon that collects and stores logs in a binary format (`/var/log/journal/`).\n2. It provides structured logging, indexing, and integration with `systemctl` and `journalctl`.\n3. journald stores structured journal entries and supports filtering by unit, boot, priority, time, and fields.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `journald` is systemd's logging daemon that collects and stores logs in a binary format (`/var/log/journal/`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `journald` is systemd's logging daemon that collects and stores logs in a binary format (`/var/log/journal/`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
