    shortDescription: "Explain configuration management.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAnsible is an agentless configuration management tool that uses YAML playbooks to define infrastructure as code. It connects to nodes via SSH and pushes configuration changes, making it simple and secure. Ansible is primarily agentless for Unix-like targets using SSH, while Windows commonly uses WinRM or PSRP. Idempotent modules are preferred over arbitrary shell commands so repeated playbook runs converge on the intended state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain Ansible and how does it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Ansible is an agentless configuration management tool that uses YAML playbooks to define infrastructure as code.\n2. It connects to nodes via SSH and pushes configuration changes, making it simple and secure.\n3. Ansible is primarily agentless for Unix-like targets using SSH, while Windows commonly uses WinRM or PSRP.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Ansible is an agentless configuration management tool that uses YAML playbooks to define infrastructure as code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Ansible is an agentless configuration management tool that uses YAML playbooks to define infrastructure as code.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
