    question: "What is the difference between active-passive and active-active clustering?",
    slug: "active-passive-vs-active-active",
    shortDescription: "Compare cluster modes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nActive-passive: one node is active, the other is on standby (takes over on failure). Active-active: all nodes are active, sharing the load. Active-passive is simpler; active-active provides better utilization but requires shared storage or replication. Active-passive needs reliable failure detection and promotion/failover, while active-active requires concurrent nodes to safely serve traffic. Stateful active-active designs often need replication, conflict handling, consistent locking, or partition-aware application logic.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between active-passive and active-active clustering and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Active-passive: one node is active, the other is on standby (takes over on failure).\n2. Active-active: all nodes are active, sharing the load.\n3. Active-passive is simpler; active-active provides better utilization but requires shared storage or replication.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Active-passive: one node is active, the other is on standby (takes over on failure).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Active-passive: one node is active, the other is on standby (takes over on failure).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
