    question: "What is the difference between DRBD and GlusterFS?",
    slug: "drbd-vs-glusterfs",
    shortDescription: "Compare storage solutions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDRBD (Distributed Replicated Block Device) provides block-level replication between two nodes, similar to RAID 1 over a network. GlusterFS is a distributed filesystem that aggregates storage from multiple nodes into a single namespace, suitable for large-scale storage. DRBD replicates block devices between nodes and is commonly paired with a filesystem and cluster manager. GlusterFS presents a distributed filesystem namespace over multiple storage nodes. They operate at different layers and solve different storage problems.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between drbd and glusterfs and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. DRBD (Distributed Replicated Block Device) provides block-level replication between two nodes, similar to RAID 1 over a network.\n2. GlusterFS is a distributed filesystem that aggregates storage from multiple nodes into a single namespace, suitable for large-scale storage.\n3. DRBD replicates block devices between nodes and is commonly paired with a filesystem and cluster manager.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. DRBD (Distributed Replicated Block Device) provides block-level replication between two nodes, similar to RAID 1 over a network.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. DRBD (Distributed Replicated Block Device) provides block-level replication between two nodes, similar to RAID 1 over a network.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
