    question: "How do you set up a high-availability cluster with Pacemaker and Corosync?",
    slug: "pacemaker-corosync",
    shortDescription: "Explain cluster resource management.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCorosync provides the messaging layer for cluster communication. Pacemaker is the cluster resource manager that controls failover. Resources (IP addresses, services, storage) are defined as resources. Pacemaker monitors them and moves them to another node in case of failure. Corosync provides cluster communication and membership/quorum information, while Pacemaker decides where and when managed resources should run. Production HA also needs fencing/STONITH and carefully defined resource constraints to prevent split-brain and unsafe dual ownership.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you set up a high-availability cluster with pacemaker and corosync, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Corosync provides the messaging layer for cluster communication.\n2. Pacemaker is the cluster resource manager that controls failover.\n3. Resources (IP addresses, services, storage) are defined as resources.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Corosync provides the messaging layer for cluster communication.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Corosync provides the messaging layer for cluster communication.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
