    slug: "ebpf",
    shortDescription: "Explain the extended Berkeley Packet Filter.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\neBPF is a Linux kernel technology that allows running sandboxed programs in kernel space without changing kernel source code. It is used for performance monitoring, networking, security (e.g., Cilium, Falco), and tracing. eBPF programs are attached to kernel events (e.g., syscalls, network packets). eBPF programs are verified before being loaded and can attach to supported hooks such as tracepoints, kprobes, networking hooks, and cgroup hooks. Modern tooling uses eBPF for observability, networking, and security without modifying the kernel source for every instrumentation task.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain eBPF and how is it used in Linux accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. eBPF is a Linux kernel technology that allows running sandboxed programs in kernel space without changing kernel source code.\n2. It is used for performance monitoring, networking, security (e.g., Cilium, Falco), and tracing.\n3. eBPF programs are attached to kernel events (e.g., syscalls, network packets).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. eBPF is a Linux kernel technology that allows running sandboxed programs in kernel space without changing kernel source code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. eBPF is a Linux kernel technology that allows running sandboxed programs in kernel space without changing kernel source code.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
