    question: "What is the difference between a bridge, a bond, and a team in Linux networking?",
    slug: "bridge-bond-team",
    shortDescription: "Compare network interface types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA bridge is a virtual switch that connects multiple network interfaces at Layer 2. A bond (bonding) combines multiple physical interfaces for redundancy or performance (mode 0-6). Team is a newer alternative to bonding with better features and management. A bridge behaves like a software Layer-2 switch, while bonding combines interfaces into one logical link for redundancy or throughput. Linux bonding is established and widely supported; the historical `team` approach is not universally preferred on modern distributions, so the platform's supported networking stack matters.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a bridge, a bond, and a team in linux networking and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A bridge is a virtual switch that connects multiple network interfaces at Layer 2.\n2. A bond (bonding) combines multiple physical interfaces for redundancy or performance (mode 0-6).\n3. Team is a newer alternative to bonding with better features and management.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A bridge is a virtual switch that connects multiple network interfaces at Layer 2.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A bridge is a virtual switch that connects multiple network interfaces at Layer 2.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
