    slug: "vxlan",
    shortDescription: "Explain virtual network encapsulation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nVXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is a network virtualization technology that encapsulates Layer 2 frames in UDP packets. It allows creating overlay networks across multiple hosts, overcoming the limitations of VLANs (4096 IDs). Linux supports VXLAN via the kernel module. VXLAN encapsulates an inner Ethernet frame in UDP, commonly using UDP port 4789. A 24-bit VNI provides a much larger logical network identifier space than traditional 12-bit VLAN IDs, making it useful for overlays across routed underlays.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain VXLAN work in Linux accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is a network virtualization technology that encapsulates Layer 2 frames in UDP packets.\n2. It allows creating overlay networks across multiple hosts, overcoming the limitations of VLANs (4096 IDs).\n3. Linux supports VXLAN via the kernel module.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is a network virtualization technology that encapsulates Layer 2 frames in UDP packets.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. VXLAN (Virtual Extensible LAN) is a network virtualization technology that encapsulates Layer 2 frames in UDP packets.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
