    question: "What is the difference between full, incremental, and differential backups?",
    slug: "backup-types",
    shortDescription: "Explain backup strategies.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFull backup: copies all data. Incremental backup: copies only changes since the last backup (full or incremental). Differential backup: copies changes since the last full backup. Incremental backups save space but are slower to restore; differential backups are faster to restore but use more space. The restore chain matters as much as backup volume: a full backup can stand alone, while an incremental chain may require the full backup plus every required incremental. Differential backups require the full backup plus the latest differential. Backups should also be tested through actual restores.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between full, incremental, and differential backups and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Full backup: copies all data.\n2. Incremental backup: copies only changes since the last backup (full or incremental).\n3. Differential backup: copies changes since the last full backup.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Full backup: copies all data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Full backup: copies all data.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
