    question: "What is the purpose of the `/etc/sudoers` file and how do you edit it safely?",
    slug: "sudoers",
    shortDescription: "Explain sudo configuration.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`/etc/sudoers` defines which users can run which commands with sudo. It should be edited using `visudo` to check syntax and prevent errors. Entries specify users, hosts, commands, and options (e.g., `NOPASSWD`). A sudoers rule combines identities, hosts, commands, and options. `visudo` performs syntax validation and can edit the correct file safely; granular command rules are preferable to broad `ALL=(ALL) ALL` access when practical.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the purpose of the `/etc/sudoers` file and how do you edit it safely accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `/etc/sudoers` defines which users can run which commands with sudo.\n2. It should be edited using `visudo` to check syntax and prevent errors.\n3. Entries specify users, hosts, commands, and options (e.g., `NOPASSWD`).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `/etc/sudoers` defines which users can run which commands with sudo.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `/etc/sudoers` defines which users can run which commands with sudo.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
