    question: "What is the difference between `yum` and `apt`?",
    slug: "yum-vs-apt",
    shortDescription: "Compare package managers.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora. `apt` (Advanced Package Tool) is the package manager for Debian/Ubuntu. Both handle dependency resolution and package installation, but use different package formats (RPM vs DEB). `apt` is the high-level package-management interface used with Debian-family packages, while modern RHEL-family systems commonly use `dnf` as the successor to `yum`. The important distinction is package ecosystem and repository metadata, not just command syntax.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `yum` and `apt` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora.\n2. `apt` (Advanced Package Tool) is the package manager for Debian/Ubuntu.\n3. Both handle dependency resolution and package installation, but use different package formats (RPM vs DEB).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora. `apt` (Advanced Package Tool) is the package manager for Debian/Ubuntu. Both handle dependency resolution and package installation, but use different package formats (RPM vs DEB). `apt` is the high-level package-management interface used with Debian-family packages, while modern RHEL-family systems commonly use `dnf` as the successor to `yum`. The important distinction is package ecosystem and repository metadata, not just command syntax.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `yum` and `apt` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora.\n2. `apt` (Advanced Package Tool) is the package manager for Debian/Ubuntu.\n3. Both handle dependency resolution and package installation, but use different package formats (RPM vs DEB).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `yum` (Yellowdog Updater Modified) is the package manager for RHEL/CentOS/Fedora.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
