    question: "What is the difference between `nice` and `renice`?",
    slug: "nice-vs-renice",
    shortDescription: "Explain process priority management.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`nice` sets the priority of a new process (`nice -n 10 command`). `renice` changes the priority of an existing process (`renice -n 10 -p PID`). Higher nice values mean lower priority (range -20 to 19). Nice values influence scheduler priority among ordinary tasks; a higher nice value generally means lower CPU scheduling priority. Moving toward negative nice values requires additional privilege, and changing priority does not guarantee a process will receive a specific percentage of CPU.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `nice` and `renice` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `nice` sets the priority of a new process (`nice -n 10 command`).\n2. `renice` changes the priority of an existing process (`renice -n 10 -p PID`).\n3. Higher nice values mean lower priority (range -20 to 19).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `nice` sets the priority of a new process (`nice -n 10 command`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `nice` sets the priority of a new process (`nice -n 10 command`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
