    shortDescription: "Walk through systematic diagnostics.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1. Check load average (`uptime`). 2. Check CPU usage (`top`, `mpstat`). 3. Check memory usage (`free -h`, `vmstat`). 4. Check disk I/O (`iostat`, `iotop`). 5. Check network (`iftop`, `nethogs`). 6. Check system logs (`journalctl`, `dmesg`). 7. Identify the bottleneck and take corrective action. The key is to establish whether the bottleneck is CPU, memory/reclaim, storage I/O, network, scheduler contention, or an application dependency. Capture evidence before and during the slowdown so the corrective action can be tied to an observed constraint.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question is useful because walk through systematic diagnostics. The interviewer can then probe the mechanism, limitation, or practical consequence behind that answer.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1.\n2. Check load average (`uptime`).\n3. 2.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. 1.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 1.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
