    slug: "disk-space-full",
    shortDescription: "Explain cleanup and expansion.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1. Find large files/directories (`du -sh /* | sort -h`). 2. Clear old logs (`logrotate`, `journalctl --vacuum-size`). 3. Remove temporary files. 4. Expand the filesystem (LVM) or add a new disk. Separate filesystem capacity from inode exhaustion: `df -h` shows blocks while `df -i` shows inodes. Before deleting files, identify what owns the data and whether logs, deleted-but-open files, container layers, snapshots, or reserved space explain the usage.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain A server is running out of disk space. How do you handle it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1.\n2. Find large files/directories (`du -sh /* | sort -h`).\n3. 2.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. 1.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 1.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
