    slug: "cron-job-not-running",
    shortDescription: "Explain cron debugging.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1. Check crontab syntax (`crontab -l`). 2. Check cron logs (`/var/log/syslog` or `journalctl -u cron`). 3. Ensure the command runs manually. 4. Check file permissions and PATH. 5. Check for environment differences. Cron jobs run with a restricted environment and may have a different working directory, PATH, shell, and permissions than an interactive session. Redirect stdout/stderr to logs and run the exact command under the same user to reproduce the failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain A cron job is not running. How do you troubleshoot it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1.\n2. Check crontab syntax (`crontab -l`).\n3. 2.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. 1.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 1.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
