    question: "A process is consuming 100% CPU. How do you handle it?",
    slug: "high-cpu-process",
    shortDescription: "Explain process troubleshooting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1. Identify the process (`top`, `ps aux`). 2. Check if it's stuck in a loop or infinite recursion. 3. Use `strace` to trace system calls. 4. Use `gdb` to attach and debug. 5. If necessary, kill the process (`kill -9 PID`). Before killing a high-CPU process, identify its command line, owner, service unit, and whether the load is expected. Profilers such as `perf`, application-specific profilers, or targeted tracing can reveal the hot path; SIGTERM is generally preferable to SIGKILL when termination is required.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain A process is consuming 100% CPU. How do you handle it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1.\n2. Identify the process (`top`, `ps aux`).\n3. 2.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. 1.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 1.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
