    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1. Check local network interface (`ip a`). 2. Check default gateway (`ip r`). 3. Check firewall rules (`iptables -L`). 4. Check if the server is powered on. 5. Check physical connectivity. Failure to ping does not prove a host is down because ICMP may be filtered. Test the actual service port, inspect routes and ARP/neighbor state, and use `traceroute`/`mtr` where appropriate. If possible, check the server locally through console access.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain A server is not responding to ping. How do you diagnose it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1.\n2. Check local network interface (`ip a`).\n3. 2.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. 1.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 1.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
