    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n1. Check CPU, memory, and I/O usage. 2. Optimize queries (add indexes). 3. Tune database configuration (buffer pool, cache size). 4. Consider vertical scaling (more RAM, faster disks) or horizontal scaling (read replicas). Tune from measurements: identify slow queries, lock contention, CPU/memory pressure, storage latency, connection saturation, and cache behavior. Indexes and configuration changes should be validated with query plans and workload measurements rather than applied generically.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain A database server is slow. How do you tune it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 1.\n2. Check CPU, memory, and I/O usage.\n3. 2.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. 1.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 1.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
