    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nClassification predicts discrete labels (e.g., spam/not spam). Regression predicts continuous values (e.g., house price).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between classification and regression and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Classification predicts discrete labels (e.g., spam/not spam).\n2. Regression predicts continuous values (e.g., house price).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Classification predicts discrete labels (e.g., spam/not spam).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Classification predicts discrete labels (e.g., spam/not spam).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
