    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHyperparameters are settings that are not learned from data, but set before training (e.g., learning rate, number of trees, regularization strength).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Hyperparameters are settings that are not learned from data, but set before training (e.g., learning rate, number of trees, regularization strength).\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Hyperparameters are settings that are not learned from data, but set before training (e.g., learning rate, number of trees, regularization strength).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn an ML workflow, what are hyperparameters matters when data quality, model behavior, evaluation, or production cost is affected. The method should be validated on representative data rather than inferred from training results alone.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
