    slug: "bagging-vs-boosting",
    shortDescription: "Compare ensemble methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBagging (e.g., Random Forest) trains models in parallel and averages predictions to reduce variance. Boosting (e.g., AdaBoost) trains sequentially, each model focuses on previous errors to reduce bias.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between bagging and boosting and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Bagging (e.g., Random Forest) trains models in parallel and averages predictions to reduce variance.\n2. Boosting (e.g., AdaBoost) trains sequentially, each model focuses on previous errors to reduce bias.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Bagging (e.g., Random Forest) trains models in parallel and averages predictions to reduce variance.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Bagging (e.g., Random Forest) trains models in parallel and averages predictions to reduce variance.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
