    slug: "clustering-vs-classification",
    shortDescription: "Compare unsupervised vs supervised.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nClustering is unsupervised (no labels) and discovers groups. Classification is supervised (labeled) and predicts labels.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between clustering and classification and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Clustering is unsupervised (no labels) and discovers groups.\n2. Classification is supervised (labeled) and predicts labels.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Clustering is unsupervised (no labels) and discovers groups.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Clustering is unsupervised (no labels) and discovers groups.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
