    question: "What is the difference between training and inference?",
    slug: "training-vs-inference",
    shortDescription: "Compare training and inference.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTraining is the process of learning model parameters from data. Inference is using the trained model to make predictions on new data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between training and inference and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Training is the process of learning model parameters from data.\n2. Inference is using the trained model to make predictions on new data.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Training is the process of learning model parameters from data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Training is the process of learning model parameters from data.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
