    shortDescription: "List and explain comparison operators.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nComparison operators: `$eq` (equal), `$ne` (not equal), `$gt` (>), `$gte` (>=), `$lt` (<), `$lte` (<=), `$in` (matches any in array), `$nin` (not in array). Example: `{ age: { $gt: 18 } }` finds documents where age > 18.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Comparison operators: `$eq` (equal), `$ne` (not equal), `$gt` (>), `$gte` (>=), `$lt` (<), `$lte` (<=), `$in` (matches any in array), `$nin` (not in array).\n2. Example: `{ age: { $gt: 18 } }` finds documents where age > 18.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Comparison operators: `$eq` (equal), `$ne` (not equal), `$gt` (>), `$gte` (>=), `$lt` (<), `$lte` (<=), `$in` (matches any in array), `$nin` (not in array).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. Comparison operators: `$eq` (equal), `$ne` (not equal), `$gt` (>), `$gte` (>=), `$lt` (<), `$lte` (<=), `$in` (matches any in array), `$nin` (not in array).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
