    shortDescription: "Compare update methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`updateOne` only updates and returns a WriteResult. `findOneAndUpdate` updates and returns the document (either before or after update). It also allows sorting and projection.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `findoneandupdate` and `updateone` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `updateOne` only updates and returns a WriteResult.\n2. `findOneAndUpdate` updates and returns the document (either before or after update).\n3. It also allows sorting and projection.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `updateOne` only updates and returns a WriteResult.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `updateOne` only updates and returns a WriteResult.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
