    question: "What is the difference between a covered query and a non-covered query?",
    slug: "covered-query",
    shortDescription: "Explain covered queries.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA query is covered if all the fields in the query and projection are part of an index, and no document scanning is needed. It returns results directly from the index, improving performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a covered query and a non-covered query and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A query is covered if all the fields in the query and projection are part of an index, and no document scanning is needed.\n2. It returns results directly from the index, improving performance.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A query is covered if all the fields in the query and projection are part of an index, and no document scanning is needed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A query is covered if all the fields in the query and projection are part of an index, and no document scanning is needed.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
