    shortDescription: "List anti-patterns.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMassive arrays that exceed 16MB (use referencing). Too many indexes. Embedding large, unbounded arrays. Using separate collections for each logical entity without good reason. Not using `_id` effectively.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Massive arrays that exceed 16MB (use referencing).\n2. Too many indexes.\n3. Embedding large, unbounded arrays.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Massive arrays that exceed 16MB (use referencing).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are the anti-patterns in mongodb schema design matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
