    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTypically, use referencing with an array of foreign keys on either side or a junction collection (like in SQL). In MongoDB, you can store an array of references on one side, and query using `$lookup` to join.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you handle many-to-many relationships, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Typically, use referencing with an array of foreign keys on either side or a junction collection (like in SQL).\n2. In MongoDB, you can store an array of references on one side, and query using `$lookup` to join.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Typically, use referencing with an array of foreign keys on either side or a junction collection (like in SQL).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Typically, use referencing with an array of foreign keys on either side or a junction collection (like in SQL).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
