    slug: "replica-set",
    shortDescription: "Define replica set.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA replica set is a group of MongoDB servers that maintain the same data set, providing redundancy and high availability. It consists of a primary node (handles writes) and secondary nodes (replicate from primary).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a replica set accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A replica set is a group of MongoDB servers that maintain the same data set, providing redundancy and high availability.\n2. It consists of a primary node (handles writes) and secondary nodes (replicate from primary).\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A replica set is a group of MongoDB servers that maintain the same data set, providing redundancy and high availability.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A replica set is a group of MongoDB servers that maintain the same data set, providing redundancy and high availability.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
