    shortDescription: "Explain automatic failover.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIf the primary fails, the secondary nodes detect the failure (after heartbeat timeouts) and hold an election to select a new primary. The election ensures that the new primary has the most recent data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain failover work in a replica set accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. If the primary fails, the secondary nodes detect the failure (after heartbeat timeouts) and hold an election to select a new primary.\n2. The election ensures that the new primary has the most recent data.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. If the primary fails, the secondary nodes detect the failure (after heartbeat timeouts) and hold an election to select a new primary.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. If the primary fails, the secondary nodes detect the failure (after heartbeat timeouts) and hold an election to select a new primary.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
