    shortDescription: "Compare roles.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPrimary accepts all write operations; secondaries replicate data from the primary and can serve reads if configured. Primary is elected; secondaries can become primary in failover.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a secondary and a primary and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Primary accepts all write operations; secondaries replicate data from the primary and can serve reads if configured.\n2. Primary is elected; secondaries can become primary in failover.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Primary accepts all write operations; secondaries replicate data from the primary and can serve reads if configured.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Primary accepts all write operations; secondaries replicate data from the primary and can serve reads if configured.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
