    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA chunk is a contiguous range of shard key values. The balancer redistributes chunks across shards to maintain balance. Chunks can be split when they grow too large.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a chunk in sharding accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A chunk is a contiguous range of shard key values.\n2. The balancer redistributes chunks across shards to maintain balance.\n3. Chunks can be split when they grow too large.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A chunk is a contiguous range of shard key values.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A chunk is a contiguous range of shard key values.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
