    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nHashed sharding (distributes data based on hash of shard key, ensures even distribution). Ranged sharding (based on ranges of values, useful for range queries). Zone sharding for geographic distribution.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Hashed sharding (distributes data based on hash of shard key, ensures even distribution).\n2. Ranged sharding (based on ranges of values, useful for range queries).\n3. Zone sharding for geographic distribution.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Hashed sharding (distributes data based on hash of shard key, ensures even distribution).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are the different sharding strategies matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
