    question: "What is the difference between write concern and transactions?",
    slug: "write-concern-vs-transaction",
    shortDescription: "Compare durability vs atomicity.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWrite concern ensures durability (data is persisted across nodes). Transactions provide atomicity (all-or-nothing) across multiple operations. They are complementary.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between write concern and transactions and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Write concern ensures durability (data is persisted across nodes).\n2. Transactions provide atomicity (all-or-nothing) across multiple operations.\n3. They are complementary.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Write concern ensures durability (data is persisted across nodes).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Write concern ensures durability (data is persisted across nodes).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
