    shortDescription: "Explain isolation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTransactions use snapshot isolation. Each transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the data. Writes are not visible to other transactions until commit. Conflicts are detected and transactions may abort.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain MongoDB ensure isolation in transactions accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Transactions use snapshot isolation.\n2. Each transaction sees a consistent snapshot of the data.\n3. Writes are not visible to other transactions until commit.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Transactions use snapshot isolation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Transactions use snapshot isolation.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
