    shortDescription: "Compare scans.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCollection scan reads every document in the collection (slow). Index scan reads only index entries and retrieves matching documents (fast). Covered queries avoid document retrieval.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between collection scan and index scan and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Collection scan reads every document in the collection (slow).\n2. Index scan reads only index entries and retrieves matching documents (fast).\n3. Covered queries avoid document retrieval.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Collection scan reads every document in the collection (slow).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Collection scan reads every document in the collection (slow).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
